(PRC-69 — `the clock's elapsed in milliseconds` reports TRUE milliseconds,
 which is what makes the supervisor's 5000-millisecond deadline fire at
 the five-second mark rather than at some scaled multiple of it.
 A 1200-millisecond wait is checked against a band rather than an exact
 value: scheduling jitter is real, a wrong UNIT would be off by a factor
 of a thousand, and the band separates those two cases without pretending
 the clock is exact.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-69.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   elapsed reports true milliseconds
)
a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 1200 milliseconds.
a number called 'milliseconds waited' is the clock's elapsed in milliseconds.
If 'milliseconds waited' is greater than or equal to 1150 and 'milliseconds waited' is less than or equal to 1500 then, print "elapsed reports true milliseconds".
If 'milliseconds waited' is less than 1150 then, print "elapsed was too small: {'milliseconds waited'}".
If 'milliseconds waited' is greater than 1500 then, print "elapsed was too large: {'milliseconds waited'}".
